{
  "issues": [
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-011",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "AC-011 claims MonoklError::Json is constructed via #[from] at 'three distinct call sites.' Grepping the verbatim source across docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md and docs/spec/03-multi-language-platform.md finds 8 raw serde_json call sites, not 3: cache-load parse (persist::load_cache, line 1602 — 1 site), cache-write serialize (persist::flush and persist::evict_lru, lines 1665/1690/1698 — 3 sites), and CLI/projection-output serialize (projection::project_inspect_result/project_search_response at 03-multi:1594/1602, output::render_json/render_json_compact at 01-core:2409/2416 — 4 sites). 'Three distinct call sites' is ambiguous: read literally against the source it is false (an implementer chasing literal compliance might wrongly consolidate call sites to force the count to 3); read loosely as 'three logical categories' it is accurate. AC-023, two criteria later, already gets this right by naming multiple files/sites per category, directly undercutting AC-011's headline count.",
      "suggested_fix": "MonoklError::Json is constructed via #[from] serde_json::Error at 8 raw call sites grouped into 3 logical categories with distinct behavioral contracts: cache-load (1 site — persist::load_cache's serde_json::from_str parsing .monokl/cache.json on load), cache-write (3 sites — persist::flush's and persist::evict_lru's serde_json::to_string_pretty calls serializing the cache back to disk), and CLI/projection-output (4 sites — projection::project_inspect_result, projection::project_search_response, output::render_json, output::render_json_compact serializing a command's result). Its Display is always exactly `json serialization failed` across all 8 sites."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-011",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "The Json variant's fixed Display text `json serialization failed` (verbatim source) is confirmed by AC-011 to cover the cache-load category too, where the actual failing operation is serde_json::from_str — a deserialize/parse failure, not serialization. SPEC-001's own Purpose promises that 'an AI coding agent parsing variants/messages at runtime to decide remediation never has to guess what a given failure means,' yet an agent reading `json serialization failed` from a cache-load site is told the wrong operation, and — per AC-023's own contract split — cannot use variant or message alone to tell the self-healed load-time case apart from the two categories that remain hard failures, since all 8 sites across all 3 categories share byte-identical Display text.",
      "suggested_fix": "Add AC-011a: 'MonoklError::Json's fixed Display text is `json parsing or serialization failed` (or equivalent wording covering both directions), never `json serialization failed` alone — the cache-load category (AC-021/AC-023) is a parse/deserialize failure and the other two categories are serialize failures; a message naming only one direction misdescribes the other.' If leaving the wording as-is is intentional, state that as an explicit non-goal with rationale rather than leaving the Purpose's 'never has to guess' promise silently unmet."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-013",
      "type": "missing-error-case",
      "description": "AC-013 states 'persist::init(), the only caller of load_cache in the spec, always intercepts this exact variant internally and never returns StaleDiskCache itself to its own caller.' This is false against the cited source: persist::flush (01-core-architecture.md:1652-1658) also calls load_cache, as a fallback when state.cache_file is None (`let mut cache = if let Some(ref existing) = state.cache_file { existing.clone() } else { load_cache(root, config_hash)? };`), and that call site propagates any error — including StaleDiskCache, and per AC-021 an uncaught Json parse error — straight out of flush() via `?`, with no interception. AC-021's self-healing is likewise scoped only to 'persist::init()', not to this second call site. A developer implementing persist.rs from this spec would reasonably read AC-013/AC-021 as guaranteeing StaleDiskCache/corrupted-Json can never reach an external caller, then be surprised when flush()'s fallback path hard-fails on exactly that condition.",
      "suggested_fix": "Rewrite AC-013's closing clause: 'persist::load_cache is called from two sites — persist::init() and, as a fallback when state.cache_file is None, persist::flush(). persist::init() always intercepts StaleDiskCache internally via its existing match arm and never returns it to its own caller. persist::flush()'s fallback call to load_cache() has no such interception and propagates StaleDiskCache (and, per AC-021, an uncaught cache-load Json error) directly to flush()'s caller via `?`. This spec's caller contract therefore requires: flush() must never be called before init() has returned Ok for the same (root, config_hash) — callers that violate this precondition observe unmediated StaleDiskCache/Json errors, and no code path currently enforces the precondition at compile time.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-015",
