{
  "id": "SPEC-003",
  "purpose": "Define the complete, compilable, verbatim shape and observable serde contract of SymbolKind, Visibility, and SymbolEntry -- monokl's core symbol-classification types, per docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md Section 4 (lines 263-310) -- so a developer implementing types.rs, or any downstream module (the TS analyzer, the Rust analyzer, or any symbols/inspect/refs/definition command) constructing or reading a SymbolEntry, never has to guess a variant, field name, field order, derive, or serde attribute, and never has to ask whether impl_owner or Visibility::Super are valid. This spec locks that canonical shape as the single source of truth these types must have, and states -- with independently re-verified citation -- that docs/spec/04-analysis-fidelity.md's impl_owner field name and Visibility::Super enum variant are wrong by construction: naming defects that would not compile against the enum/struct locked here, not stylistic variance to reconcile later.",
  "scope": "SymbolKind (all 15 variants, in declaration order, its two-line derive/attribute header, and its camelCase JSON serialization for every variant), Visibility (all 4 variants, in declaration order, its derive header, its camelCase JSON serialization, and its explicit non-existence of a 5th Super variant), and SymbolEntry (all 8 fields, their exact types and declaration order, its own two-line derive/attribute header -- notably lacking Copy/PartialEq/Eq unlike its two constituent enums -- the per-field #[serde(...)] attribute that four of its eight fields carry and four don't, and the resulting camelCase JSON key spellings including traitImpl and kindDetail), verbatim per 01-core-architecture.md:263-310, cross-checked against 03-multi-language-platform.md:2772-2797's independent restatement of the same three types (found identical, confirming no drift between the two source documents on these types' shape). It also locks the observable deserialize behavior of all three types under malformed, incomplete, or out-of-range JSON input -- missing vs. null vs. omitted optional fields, unrecognized enum-variant strings, and unrecognized extra object keys -- since that is exactly the kind of decision a spec must pin down for a developer or agent who has not seen the implementation. Finally, it states the canonical-vs-defective naming contract for owner (not impl_owner) and Visibility's four variants (no Super), citing docs/spec/07-edge-cases-and-failure-modes.md Part 4 findings #1 and #2, which this spec independently re-confirms were not among the five findings that document's own adversarial verification pass weakened, nor its one struck-through finding.",
