      "description": "AC-005's central error-case claim is empirically false. It states: 'A JSON object missing the \"signature\" key entirely fails to deserialize (no #[serde(default)] on that field to supply a fallback).' Compiling the exact SymbolEntry/SymbolKind/Visibility shape locked by AC-001/AC-003/AC-004 and deserializing `{\"name\":\"foo\",\"kind\":\"function\",\"line\":10}` (signature key entirely absent) via serde_json succeeds, producing `signature: None` — verified by running it. serde_derive special-cases any field syntactically typed `Option<T>`: such a field is treated as implicitly optional on deserialize (missing key -> None) regardless of whether `#[serde(default)]` is present. So signature behaves identically to owner/trait_impl/visibility/kind_detail on the deserialize side; the `#[serde(default)]` on the latter four is redundant for that purpose. AC-005's serialize-side claim (None signature -> key present as null; None on the other four -> key omitted) is correct and was independently verified — only the deserialize-side 'fails to deserialize' claim is wrong. Since this AC is flagged is_error_case:true, its entire job is to pin down error/edge-case deserialize behavior for TDD fixtures, and a test written against the stated claim would encode incorrect expected behavior.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace the deserialize-side claim with: 'All five Option-typed fields (signature, owner, trait_impl, visibility, kind_detail) deserialize leniently when their JSON key is missing: serde treats any field syntactically typed Option<T> as implicitly defaulted to None on a missing key, independent of an explicit #[serde(default)] attribute. A JSON object omitting \"signature\" deserializes successfully with signature: None, exactly like omitting owner/traitImpl/visibility/kindDetail. The #[serde(default)] on the latter four has no deserialization effect beyond what Option<T> already provides implicitly — it is present alongside skip_serializing_if as an idiomatic pairing, not because it changes missing-key handling. The only real signature-vs-other-four asymmetry is on the SERIALIZE side (see next AC).'"
