{
  "id": "SPEC-003",
  "purpose": "Define the complete, compilable, verbatim shape and observable serde contract of SymbolKind, Visibility, and SymbolEntry -- monokl's core symbol-classification types, per docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md Section 4 (lines 263-310) -- so a developer implementing types.rs, or any downstream module (the TS analyzer, the Rust analyzer, or any symbols/inspect/refs/definition command) constructing or reading a SymbolEntry, never has to guess a variant, field name, field order, derive, or serde attribute, and never has to ask whether impl_owner or Visibility::Super are valid. This spec locks that canonical shape as the single source of truth these types must have, and states -- with independently re-verified citation -- that docs/spec/04-analysis-fidelity.md's impl_owner field name and Visibility::Super enum variant describe an implementation that, if written as literally specified there, would not compile against the types locked here.",
  "scope": "SymbolKind (all 15 variants, in declaration order, its two-line derive/attribute header, and its camelCase JSON serialization for every variant), Visibility (all 4 variants, in declaration order, its derive header, its camelCase JSON serialization, and its explicit non-existence of a 5th Super variant), and SymbolEntry (all 8 fields, their exact types and declaration order, its own two-line derive/attribute header -- notably lacking Copy/PartialEq/Eq unlike its two constituent enums -- the per-field #[serde(...)] attribute that four of its eight fields carry and four don't, and the resulting camelCase JSON key spellings including traitImpl and kindDetail), verbatim per 01-core-architecture.md:263-310, cross-checked against 03-multi-language-platform.md:2772-2797's independent restatement and against 02-inspection-and-analysis.md's two independent restatements (Visibility at lines 150-158, SymbolKind at lines 1779-1794) -- all found identical, confirming no drift across the four locations these types appear. It also locks the observable deserialize behavior of all three types under malformed, incomplete, or out-of-range JSON input -- missing vs. null vs. omitted optional fields, unrecognized enum-variant strings, and unrecognized extra object keys -- since that is exactly the kind of decision a spec must pin down for a developer or agent who has not seen the implementation. Finally, it states the canonical-vs-defective naming contract for owner (not impl_owner) and Visibility's four variants (no Super), citing docs/spec/07-edge-cases-and-failure-modes.md Part 4 findings #1 and #2, independently re-confirmed as carrying neither a \"Verified correction\" annotation nor a strike-through from that document's own single adversarial verification pass.",
  "non_goals": [
    "All other types declared in Section 4 of 01-core-architecture.md (DependencyRecord, DependencyBinding, BindingKind, DependencyTarget, RustPathAnchor, CodeBlock, RankedBlock, ParentContext, SearchOptions, SearchLimits, Language, Diagnostic, DiagnosticKind, LangData and its per-language data structs, ExportRecord, JsxAttribute, JsxElementEntry, SearchResponse, SymbolsResult, DependentsResult, LineHit, TsconfigMode, WorkspaceOptions, ExtractRequest) -- each is a separate track; only SymbolKind, Visibility, and SymbolEntry are locked here.",
    "Implementing or correcting docs/spec/04-analysis-fidelity.md's rust_visibility(node) or extract_impl_symbols functions themselves (04-analysis-fidelity.md:42-103) -- that is the Rust-fidelity-fix track's job. This track only locks the canonical types those functions must eventually conform to.",
    "The Part 4 finding #3 signature-normalization discrepancy (07-edge-cases-and-failure-modes.md:140) -- whether SymbolEntry.signature should be produced by first_line_signature (truncate at the first newline, the existing behavior per 02-inspection-and-analysis.md) or 04-analysis-fidelity.md's proposed normalize_signature (whitespace-collapse across the full multi-line span). This spec locks that signature is Option<String>, present unconditionally as a JSON key on serialize (AC-005); it does not resolve which function populates its content.",
    "kind_detail's full value vocabulary -- only its type (Option<String>) and field presence/serde attributes are locked here (AC-004, AC-005). The set of valid kebab-case strings (e.g. \"rust-struct\", \"rust-impl\") is owned by each language analyzer's own track.",
    "owner's and trait_impl's population semantics -- which impl block or free-standing item produces which value, and the self-type-propagation rule from an impl block down to its associated fns -- are owned by the language-analyzer implementation track that constructs SymbolEntry values, not this type-shape track. Likewise, which language construct a given analyzer maps to SymbolKind::Other is an analyzer-level decision, not locked here beyond Other's existence as a valid variant (AC-001).",
    "Editing docs/spec/04-analysis-fidelity.md's own prose to replace impl_owner with owner and Visibility::Super with Visibility::Module -- that is a docs-consistency edit to a different file, outside a Rust-type-shape track's scope. This spec states the contradiction and its resolution (AC-007, AC-008) so that any future implementation of 04-analysis-fidelity.md must conform to the canonical types locked here; it does not itself patch that file's text.",
    "Explaining or defending the rationale for SymbolEntry's serialize-side asymmetry between signature (key always present, null if unset) and owner/trait_impl/visibility/kind_detail (key omitted if unset) -- no source document states why this asymmetry exists. AC-005 documents it as a fact to preserve, not a design choice to justify or a defect to flag; inventing a plausible-sounding rationale (e.g. backward compatibility) would violate this spec's no-invented-facts discipline."
  ],
  "api_surface": [
    {
      "name": "SymbolKind",
      "signature": "#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)] #[serde(rename_all = \"camelCase\")] pub enum SymbolKind { Function, Method, Constructor, Class, Struct, Enum, Interface, TypeAlias, Property, Field, Variable, Module, Impl, Macro, Other }",
      "description": "monokl's universal symbol-classification enum, verbatim per 01-core-architecture.md:263-284, independently restated identically at 03-multi-language-platform.md:2772-2780 and 02-inspection-and-analysis.md:1779-1794. 12 universal variants usable by any language analyzer, 2 Rust-specific variants (Impl, Macro), and 1 Other catch-all -- 15 total, in this declaration order. No #[non_exhaustive] attribute.",
      "error_conditions": [
        "Deserialize fails with an 'unknown variant' error for any JSON string not matching one of the 15 camelCase spellings in AC-002 -- see AC-009."
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Visibility",
      "signature": "#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)] #[serde(rename_all = \"camelCase\")] pub enum Visibility { Public, Crate, Module, Private }",
      "description": "monokl's symbol-visibility enum, verbatim per 01-core-architecture.md:286-293, independently restated identically at 02-inspection-and-analysis.md:150-158, and cross-checked against 02-inspection-and-analysis.md:1722-1732's parse_visibility, whose six return statements only ever construct these four variants. Exactly 4 variants, no 5th Super variant -- see AC-008 for the naming-contract statement against 04-analysis-fidelity.md's contradicting rust_visibility.",
      "error_conditions": [
        "Deserialize fails with an 'unknown variant' error for any JSON string not matching one of the 4 camelCase spellings in AC-003, including the string \"super\" that a rust_visibility implementation following 04-analysis-fidelity.md:62 literally would attempt to produce -- see AC-008."
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "SymbolEntry",
      "signature": "#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)] #[serde(rename_all = \"camelCase\")] pub struct SymbolEntry { pub name: String, pub kind: SymbolKind, pub line: usize, pub signature: Option<String>, #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")] pub owner: Option<String>, #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")] pub trait_impl: Option<String>, #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")] pub visibility: Option<Visibility>, #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")] pub kind_detail: Option<String> }",
      "description": "monokl's per-symbol output record, verbatim per 01-core-architecture.md:295-310, independently restated identically at 03-multi-language-platform.md:2786-2797 under the heading confirming SymbolEntry 'gained owner, trait_impl, visibility, kind_detail' (line 2783). Exactly 8 fields in this declaration order; derives Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize only -- notably not Copy, not PartialEq, not Eq (unlike SymbolKind and Visibility), and no #[non_exhaustive]. Field name is owner, never impl_owner -- see AC-007.",
      "error_conditions": [
        "Deserialize succeeds, defaulting every one of the five Option-typed fields (signature, owner, trait_impl, visibility, kind_detail) to None, if the JSON object omits that field's key entirely -- see AC-005.",
        "Deserialize succeeds, silently discarding any unrecognized extra key -- see AC-006.",
        "Deserialize fails if \"kind\", or \"visibility\" when present, holds an unrecognized enum-variant string -- see AC-009."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "SymbolKind (01-core-architecture.md:263-284) compiles with exactly these 15 variants, in this exact declaration order: Function, Method, Constructor, Class, Struct, Enum, Interface, TypeAlias, Property, Field, Variable, Module (12 universal variants, usable by any language analyzer), then Impl, Macro (2 Rust-specific variants), then Other (1 catch-all) -- 12 + 2 + 1 = 15. The enum carries exactly two attribute lines: #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)] and #[serde(rename_all = \"camelCase\")] (lines 263-264) -- no #[non_exhaustive]. Independently restated identically (same 15 variants, same order, same two attribute lines) at 03-multi-language-platform.md:2772-2780 and at 02-inspection-and-analysis.md:1779-1794 (\"Updated node_kind.rs -- new SymbolKind variants\") -- confirming no drift across all three locations.",
      "is_error_case": false,
      "judgment_call": false
    },
