      "suggested_fix": "docs/spec/03-multi-language-platform.md independently restates DependencyTarget and RustPathAnchor verbatim in its own 'types added' changelog block (03-multi-language-platform.md:2400-2436) and separately references DependencyTarget::Namespace / BindingKind::Namespace pattern matches (03-multi-language-platform.md:1889, 1895) and the Go/Java-replaces-CSharp LangData note (03-multi-language-platform.md:2383) -- all confirmed identical in shape to the canonical 01-core-architecture.md:312-452 source this spec locks; no drift found. This track does not claim to have checked every other document in the corpus for a further restatement of these types beyond 01-core-architecture.md and 03-multi-language-platform.md."
    }
  ],
  "overall": "fail",
  "reasoning": "SPEC-005's serde-mechanics content (internal-vs-adjacent tagging, Vec<T>-vs-Option<T> deserialize leniency, necessary-vs-redundant renames) is careful and, where checked against 01-core-architecture.md:312-452, accurate. But two classes of real defect survive: (1) AC-007's central negative claim -- that no planned analyzer constructs DependencyTarget::Namespace -- fails to reconcile with 05-research-and-decisions.md §10, which reintroduces C# (the variant's original motivating language) as a decided, adopted-but-unbuilt fallback-tier target in the very document AC-007 cites for the opposite conclusion; the claim also lacks the corpus-wide-grep citation this project's other specs (SPEC-004) use for comparable negative claims. This is a genuine incompleteness, not a style nit, since it's the kind of claim a downstream reader could rely on and be wrong. (2) The missing-required-field error case is asserted unevenly across the DependencyRecord/DependencyBinding/ExportRecord/JsxAttribute/JsxElementEntry cluster: only JsxElementEntry (AC-011) both states the observable failure and tags is_error_case:true; DependencyBinding (AC-002) states the same failure but is mistagged false; DependencyRecord's required fields (AC-001) get no failure statement at all; ExportRecord and JsxAttribute (AC-009, AC-010) state 'required' without the observable consequence. One untestable clause survives in AC-016 (an assertion about this spec's own authorial intent rather than LangData's behavior) -- a milder instance of the same 'spec-construction narrative in criterion text' pattern the brief warned to watch for, though not the historical-revision-narration form found in SPEC-003/004. A secondary non_goals inconsistency (an internal reference to 'two locations' that doesn't match the rest of the document, plus an unchecked restatement in 03-multi-language-platform.md that in fact matches and should be cited per SPEC-003's precedent) rounds out the findings. None of these invalidate the spec's core technical content, but AC-007's overclaim and the required-field coverage gaps are load-bearing enough to fail this round."
}
