    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-005",
      "type": "incomplete",
      "description": "AC-005 promises detail it never delivers: it says every field is unconditionally present in serialize output 'except as governed by... parent_context's own omit-vs-null behavior (AC-005 continues below)', but no criterion anywhere in the spec (AC-005, AC-006, or AC-007) actually states whether RankedBlock.parent_context serializes as JSON null when None or is omitted. This is inferable from AC-005's own statement that #[serde(flatten)] is 'the sole field-level attribute anywhere on this struct' (so parent_context has no skip_serializing_if and must always emit its key), but the spec dangles a forward reference instead of stating the conclusion, leaving a developer to do the inference the spec itself promised to do.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace the trailing sentence of AC-005 ('Every one of its 7 fields... AC-005 continues below.') with: 'Every one of its 7 fields (6 own fields plus the flattened CodeBlock) is unconditionally present in its serialize output except where CodeBlock's own per-field serialize attributes (AC-002) cause omission. parent_context carries no field-level serde attribute -- #[serde(flatten)] on block is the sole field-level attribute anywhere on this struct -- so a RankedBlock with parent_context: None serializes its \"parentContext\" key with a JSON null value rather than omitting the key, the same always-present-key pattern AC-002 establishes for CodeBlock.symbol_signature and AC-010 establishes for Diagnostic.path.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-003",
      "type": "missing-error-case",
      "description": "CodeBlock carries no #[serde(deny_unknown_fields)] attribute, and no criterion states what happens when a JSON object being deserialized into CodeBlock includes a key not among its 8 named fields. This is the same class of input SPEC-003 AC-006 explicitly locks for SymbolEntry ('extra keys not among the 8 named... for example, an object with an unexpected impl_owner key') -- an established pattern in this project's sibling specs that SPEC-006 silently drops for CodeBlock, the one deserializable type in this cluster with the most fields.",
      "suggested_fix": "Add to AC-003: 'CodeBlock carries no #[serde(deny_unknown_fields)] attribute anywhere in its declaration (01-core-architecture.md:454-467). Deserializing a JSON object that includes an extra key not among the 8 named in AC-001 (for example, an object with all 5 required keys plus an unrecognized \"extraField\" key) succeeds, silently discarding the unrecognized key -- serde's derived Deserialize ignores unknown fields by default in the absence of that attribute, the same unknown-key-tolerant behavior SPEC-003 AC-006 establishes for SymbolEntry.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-010",
      "type": "missing-error-case",
      "description": "Diagnostic likewise carries no #[serde(deny_unknown_fields)] attribute, and AC-010 (the criterion that already covers Diagnostic's missing-field and leniency behavior) says nothing about extra/unrecognized keys -- the same gap as CodeBlock, for the same established sibling pattern (SPEC-003 AC-006).",
      "suggested_fix": "Add to AC-010: 'Diagnostic carries no #[serde(deny_unknown_fields)] attribute (01-core-architecture.md:490-496). Deserializing a JSON object for Diagnostic that includes an extra key beyond kind/path/message succeeds, silently discarding the unrecognized key, the same unknown-key-tolerant behavior SPEC-003 AC-006 establishes for SymbolEntry.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-003",
      "type": "missing-error-case",
      "description": "No criterion states the deserialize consequence of a negative integer supplied for CodeBlock's usize fields (line_start, line_end) or for an element of matched_lines: Vec<usize>. This is the exact class of out-of-range input SPEC-003 AC-012 already locks for SymbolEntry.line ('a JSON object where \"line\" holds a negative integer... fails to deserialize with an invalid-value error'). SPEC-006's AC-004 discusses CodeBlock's line_start/line_end invariants at length but only in the cross-field sense (line_start <= line_end, matched_lines range) -- it never states what happens when a single field's raw value is negative, which is a distinct and equally real off-nominal input for a usize-typed field.",
      "suggested_fix": "Add to AC-003: 'A JSON object where \"lineStart\" or \"lineEnd\" holds a negative integer (e.g. -1) fails to deserialize with an invalid-value error, since serde_json's integer deserialization for unsigned target types (usize) rejects negative source values -- the same out-of-range behavior SPEC-003 AC-012 establishes for SymbolEntry.line. A \"matchedLines\" array containing a negative integer element fails the same way and for the same reason, since matched_lines' element type is also usize.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-009",
      "type": "scope-overlap",
      "description": "AC-001 (CodeBlock) explicitly disclaims re-locking SymbolKind's shape for its node_kind: SymbolKind field ('this track does not re-lock SymbolKind's own shape, only that CodeBlock's fourth field has this type'). AC-009 (ParentContext) has the identical situation -- kind: SymbolKind -- but carries no such disclaimer. Without it, a reader cannot tell from AC-009 alone whether SPEC-006 is silently asserting something about SymbolKind's own shape through ParentContext, creating ambiguity about which spec owns SymbolKind's contract in this context.",
      "suggested_fix": "Append to AC-009: 'ParentContext.kind: SymbolKind references the enum locked in SPEC-003 (docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md:263-284); as with CodeBlock.node_kind (AC-001), this track does not re-lock SymbolKind's own shape, only that ParentContext's first field has this type.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-013",
      "type": "untestable",
      "description": "AC-013's core claim -- that a hypothetical future variant like PartiallyDegraded would diverge under 'lowercase' versus 'camelCase' -- cannot be checked against DiagnosticKind as actually compiled, because no such variant exists in the type this spec locks (AC-012's 3 variants are Degraded/Skipped/Warning, none multi-word). Per the auditability test, a criterion must be evaluable from the observable behavior of the system as specified; a claim about a variant this spec explicitly does not add is not falsifiable against DiagnosticKind today, no matter how correct the underlying serde-macro reasoning is. As currently written this reads as a locked, testable acceptance criterion when its divergence claim is actually an illustrative aside about serde's rename_all mechanism in general.",
      "suggested_fix": "Split AC-013 into a testable criterion and a non-binding note. Testable AC-013: 'The \"lowercase\" rule applied by DiagnosticKind's rename_all lowercases a variant name's every character uniformly, with no word-boundary handling of any kind -- a categorically different transform from \"camelCase\" (SPEC-003 AC-002, SPEC-005 AC-013). For DiagnosticKind's 3 actual variants -- Degraded, Skipped, Warning -- \"lowercase\" produces exactly \"degraded\", \"skipped\", \"warning\": this is directly verifiable by serializing each DiagnosticKind variant and comparing the output against those three exact strings.' Move the divergence illustration out of the acceptance criteria entirely into the spec's reasoning/rationale field: 'Note (non-binding, not independently checkable against this type as specified): the equivalence between \"lowercase\" and \"camelCase\" output for these 3 variants is a coincidence of their single-word names, not evidence the two rules are interchangeable in general -- a multi-word variant name, which this spec does not introduce, would diverge under the two rules.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-008",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "AC-008 states serde_json's non-finite-to-null behavior as a general fact about the library ('serde_json's f64 serialization represents a non-finite value... as the JSON literal null') rather than as a concrete, directly observable proposition about a RankedBlock instance. This is accurate and does not require reading serde_json's source to confirm, but as phrased it is a claim about a third-party library's documented behavior, not an example a tester can mechanically execute against monokl's own types -- there's a gap between 'serde_json does X in general' and 'constructing this specific RankedBlock and serializing it produces this specific JSON.' A tester should not have to independently verify serde_json's general contract; the criterion should hand them the exact, monokl-scoped check.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace the criterion's closing claim with a concrete, self-contained example: 'Constructing a RankedBlock whose bm25_score field is set to f64::NAN and serializing it with serde_json produces a JSON object whose \"bm25Score\" key holds the literal value null (not a numeric literal, and serialization does not fail or panic). The same holds for f64::INFINITY and f64::NEG_INFINITY, and for coverage_boost, node_type_boost, and final_score individually -- each of RankedBlock's four f64 scoring fields exhibits this null-on-non-finite behavior independently when serialized, since serde_json has no native JSON representation for non-finite numbers and its documented Serialize impl for f64 substitutes null rather than failing the whole call. No source document states that monokl's ranking pipeline (rank/bm25.rs, rank/boost.rs) guarantees these four fields are always finite before constructing a RankedBlock; if any of them were ever non-finite, the resulting JSON would silently contain null for that field, indistinguishable on the wire from a genuinely absent value.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-015",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "AC-015 says using LineHit where Serialize/Deserialize is required 'produces a compile-time trait-bound error, not a runtime failure' -- correct in substance, but 'a compile-time trait-bound error' doesn't specify the observable signature precisely enough for two independent testers to agree they've seen the same failure. Any of dozens of distinct rustc diagnostics could loosely be called a 'trait-bound error'; without naming what the compiler actually says, one tester could accept an unrelated compile failure (e.g. a typo) as satisfying the criterion.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace the consequence sentence in AC-015 with: 'Consequence: LineHit cannot be serialized or deserialized directly, and cannot be a field of any struct that itself derives Serialize or Deserialize without a manual custom implementation. Attempting either produces a compile-time failure, not a runtime failure or a silently-wrong JSON shape: rustc rejects the derive with an unsatisfied-trait-bound diagnostic (E0277) whose message states that the trait bound `LineHit: serde::Serialize` (or `serde::Deserialize`) is not satisfied, naming LineHit specifically as the type missing the required impl.'"
    }
  ],
  "overall": "fail",
  "reasoning": "Field/derive/attribute transcription is verbatim-correct against 01-core-architecture.md:454-603 for all six types, and the flatten key-collision claim (AC-006) checks out by direct enumeration. But five distinct problems survive scrutiny: (1) AC-005 makes a forward promise ('AC-005 continues below') about parent_context's omit-vs-null behavior that is never fulfilled anywhere in the document -- a genuine incomplete section, not just a stylistic gap, since it's the one Option field in the cluster whose serialize behavior is never stated outright. (2) Two missing-error-case gaps -- extra/unknown JSON keys on CodeBlock and Diagnostic, and negative-integer-into-usize on CodeBlock's line fields -- both of which this same project already established as required coverage via SPEC-003 AC-006 and AC-012 on the structurally analogous SymbolEntry; SPEC-006 doesn't even carry forward its own sibling's precedent for the two deserializable types in this cluster, despite lifting the SPEC-003/SPEC-005 precedent explicitly elsewhere (AC-002's Vec-vs-Option reasoning). (3) A scope-overlap inconsistency: AC-001 explicitly disclaims re-locking SymbolKind for CodeBlock.node_kind but AC-009 doesn't make the identical disclaimer for ParentContext.kind, despite the identical situation. (4) AC-013's hypothetical-variant divergence claim fails the auditability test outright -- it cannot be checked against DiagnosticKind as this spec actually locks it, since the variant that would demonstrate the divergence doesn't exist and isn't proposed. (5) AC-008 and AC-015, while factually accurate, state their key claims as general facts about serde_json/rustc rather than concrete, monokl-scoped, mechanically checkable propositions -- both are fixable with tighter phrasing but as written leave room for two testers to disagree on what would count as confirmation."
}
