{
  "verdict": "fail",
  "confidence": "high",
  "artifact_type": "spec@1",
  "retry_count": 0,
  "verdict_summary": "FAIL. Compiled verbatim lexer/parser/plan and ran every input the criteria cite; 16 of 21 verified exactly. Blockers: AC-006's double-backslash example contradicts itself, AC-020's pos-advancement claim is false at 4 sites, AC-008/AC-016 omit load-bearing pub, AC-019's universal is false.",
  "blockers": [
    {
      "criterion": "AC-006",
      "location": ".claude/specs/SPEC-009.json line 56; source docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md:716-734",
      "finding": "The four worked examples use two incompatible notations for the token payload, and example 3 is false under either reading. Examples 1/2/4 write the payload as a Rust string literal -- Token::QuotedString(\"a\\\"b\") denotes the 3 chars a,\",b and Token::QuotedString(\"a\\\\\") denotes the 2 chars a,backslash -- and both were verified correct against the compiled read_quoted(). Example 3 writes Token::QuotedString(\"a\\\\b\"), which as a Rust literal denotes the 3 chars a,backslash,b, while its own trailing prose says the payload contains \"two literal backslash characters, not one\". Ran the verbatim read_quoted() against the criterion's own stated input (6 chars: quote,a,backslash,backslash,b,quote): the payload is 4 chars a,backslash,backslash,b. Concretely, tok == Token::QuotedString(\"a\\\\b\".to_string()) evaluates FALSE and tok == Token::QuotedString(\"a\\\\\\\\b\".to_string()) evaluates TRUE. A tester transcribing the criterion's code text writes a failing assertion and must make a judgment call about which half of the criterion to believe -- the prose or the literal. FIX: (1) state the notation convention explicitly inside the criterion: \"inputs are written as raw character sequences including the opening quote; token payloads are written as Rust string literals\"; (2) correct example 3's payload to Token::QuotedString(\"a\\\\\\\\b\"), the Rust literal denoting two backslashes; (3) append an unambiguous char-count gloss to each of the four examples -- ex1 payload = 3 chars f,o,o; ex2 = 3 chars a,quote,b; ex3 = 4 chars a,backslash,backslash,b; ex4 = 2 chars a,backslash -- so the notation cannot be misread again."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "AC-020",
      "location": ".claude/specs/SPEC-009.json line 70; source docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md:724, 740, 741, 742",
      "finding": "The criterion's stated mechanism is factually false against the verbatim source. It asserts that self.pos \"is advanced exclusively by either a consumed char's own ch.len_utf8() (advance(), skip_whitespace(), read_word(), read_quoted()) or by the exact byte length of the ASCII literal \\\"regex:\\\"\". The source contains four advancement sites that use a hardcoded self.pos += 1 rather than len_utf8(): line 740 (Some('+') => { self.pos += 1; Token::Plus }), line 741 (Some('-')), line 742 (Some('\"')), and line 724 inside read_quoted()'s escape arm (self.pos += 1; immediately before s.push('\"')). The criterion also miscredits read_quoted() as len_utf8()-based when only its advance() calls are; its escape arm is one of the four literal-1 sites. The observable half of the criterion is correct and was verified on the compiled lexer -- toks(\"+cafe\" with e-acute) == [Plus, Word(cafe), Eof], parse of that input yields exactly one Required term whose pattern is byte-identical to the input word, and the Japanese quoted string tokenizes to a single QuotedString with all three chars intact -- but the char-boundary conclusion does not follow from the stated premise, because the premise is wrong. FIX: replace the \"advanced exclusively by ... ch.len_utf8() ... or by the exact byte length of the ASCII literal regex:\" clause with the accurate enumeration: pos is advanced by a consumed char's ch.len_utf8() in advance(), skip_whitespace(), and read_word(); by \"regex:\".len() after starts_with() has verified the prefix; and by a literal 1 at exactly four sites (next_token's '+', '-', and '\"' arms, and read_quoted's escaped-quote arm), each of which is guarded by a preceding peek()/match that proves the byte at pos is a 1-byte ASCII character -- which is the actual reason every increment still lands on a UTF-8 char boundary."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "AC-008, AC-016",
      "location": ".claude/specs/SPEC-009.json lines 58 and 66; source docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md:771-776, 864-870",
      "finding": "Neither criterion locks field visibility, and visibility is load-bearing. AC-008 states ParsedTerm has \"exactly 3 fields in this order: modifier: Modifier, pattern: String, is_regex: bool\" and AC-016 states QueryPlan has \"exactly 4 fields in this order: terms, required, excluded, scored\" -- neither says pub. In the verbatim source all seven fields are declared pub. This is not cosmetic: query/plan.rs is a different module from query/parser.rs and reads t.modifier (line 875) and self.terms[i].pattern (line 890) directly across that boundary. Built the four-file module tree with ParsedTerm's three fields private: it fails to compile with two errors -- error[E0616]: field `modifier` of struct `ParsedTerm` is private, and error[E0616]: field `pattern` of struct `ParsedTerm` is private. An implementer working only from AC-008 has no instruction on visibility and must guess; guessing private produces code that does not compile. AC-002 already sets the spec's own precedent by explicitly locking Lexer's two fields as private AND spelling out the consequence (\"no code outside query/lexer.rs's own impl block can read or construct a Lexer's fields directly\"), so this is an inconsistency within the spec's established standard. FIX: append to AC-008 -- \"All 3 fields are declared pub. This is required, not incidental: query/plan.rs reads t.modifier and t.pattern across a module boundary, so declaring any of the three private is an E0616 compile failure naming that field.\" Append to AC-016 -- \"All 4 fields are declared pub, so a QueryPlan can be constructed by struct literal from any module, not only via from_terms.\""
    },
    {
      "criterion": "AC-019",
      "location": ".claude/specs/SPEC-009.json line 69; source docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md:888-892",
      "finding": "The criterion asserts a universal that is false. It states that excluded-category terms \"never appear in this method's return value under any input\". Because QueryPlan's four fields are pub, a QueryPlan can be built by struct literal without passing through from_terms, and AC-017's partition invariant holds only for from_terms-built plans. Verified against the compiled code: QueryPlan { terms: parse(\"-foo\").unwrap(), required: vec![0], excluded: vec![], scored: vec![] }.search_patterns() returns [\"foo\"] -- the pattern of an Excluded term appearing in the output, directly contradicting \"under any input\". Separately, QueryPlan { terms: vec![], required: vec![5], excluded: vec![], scored: vec![] }.search_patterns() panics with an index-out-of-bounds on the unchecked self.terms[i] index at line 890; this panic path is nowhere locked, while api_surface (line 46) describes search_patterns as total and lists no error_conditions. FIX: (1) narrow AC-019's universal to read \"for any QueryPlan produced by QueryPlan::from_terms, excluded-category terms never appear in search_patterns()'s return value\"; (2) add one new criterion with is_error_case: true stating that search_patterns() indexes self.terms[i] without a bounds check, that neither from_terms nor any other code validates the required/excluded/scored index vectors against terms.len(), and that a directly-constructed QueryPlan carrying an index >= terms.len() therefore panics rather than returning an error or an empty Vec."
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "Verification method: built a throwaway crate (regex 1.x, rustc 1.96.0) containing query/lexer.rs, query/parser.rs and query/plan.rs transcribed verbatim from docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md:643-897, plus a MonoklError stub matching SPEC-001 AC-008's shape, and executed every input string the criteria cite rather than reasoning about them. Source read independently at 640-905; the spec's line citations were spot-checked and are all accurate.\n\nVERIFIED EXACTLY (16): AC-001 (derive list and 6-variant order match; separately compiled a serde::Serialize struct holding Token and confirmed the exact error is error[E0277]: the trait bound `Token: serde::Serialize` is not satisfied, naming Token -- the criterion's E0277 claim is correct). AC-002 (zero derives on Lexer confirmed; the novelty claim was independently checked by scanning every pub struct/enum in all seven spec docs for a missing derive attribute -- Lexer at line 667 is the first, all later zero-derive types (TsAnalyzer:1732, ImportGraph:2125, etc.) fall outside any prior spec's locked range, and LineHit:599, TsconfigMode:605, WorkspaceOptions:612 each carry #[derive(Debug, Clone)] exactly as AC-002 asserts). AC-003 (6 private helpers, 2 public methods). AC-004/AC-005 (dispatch order confirmed; \"foo+bar\" -> [Word(\"foo+bar\"), Eof], \"REGEX:foo\" -> [Word(\"REGEX:foo\")] confirming case-sensitivity, \"regex\" without colon -> Word). AC-007 (\"+foo\"/\"+ foo\" and \"-foo\"/\"- foo\" each produce identical single Required/Excluded terms -- Plus/Minus parity holds). AC-009 (the NBSP divergence claim is correct in every part: '\\u{a0}'.is_whitespace()==true but is_ascii_whitespace()==false; parse(\"\\u{a0}\")==Ok([]) via early return; parse(\"\\u{a0}foo\") yields one Optional term whose pattern is the NBSP-prefixed word verbatim, confirming regex::escape leaves NBSP unchanged; U+2003 and U+3000 also early-return). AC-010 (content_to_term returns None for RegexPrefix/Plus/Minus/Eof; parse(\"+\")==Ok([])). AC-011 (\"regex: -foo\" -> exactly one Optional non-regex term \"foo\"; the Minus is permanently consumed with no diagnostic, exactly as traced; parse(\"regex:\")==Ok([]); \"regex: +foo\" and \"regex: regex:foo\" behave identically). AC-012. AC-013 (both \"+regex:foo\" and \"-regex:foo\" degrade to a single Optional escaped-literal \"foo\" -- finding #8's characterization is correct and the Plus/Minus parity claim holds). AC-014 (63->Ok, 64->Ok len 64, 65->Err TooManyTerms{count:65,limit:64}, 66->Err count:66; boundary is exactly as specified). AC-015 (parse(\"regex:(unclosed\") succeeds with is_regex:true and pattern \"(unclosed\" while Regex::new(\"(unclosed\") errors). AC-017 (three-way partition is total and disjoint; order preserved). AC-018 (\"-foo -bar\" gives is_empty()==false with search_patterns()==[]). AC-021 (mod.rs matches exactly: 3 pub mod, 2 pub use, correct order and grouping).\n\nThe four blockers are all defects the canon-auditor pass did not catch, and two of them are in text the auditor itself introduced -- AC-006's worked examples and AC-020's new multi-byte criterion. That is worth noting for the drafter: the added material was not itself executed before being added. AC-006 and AC-020 are the serious ones; both state propositions that a tester checking against real code finds false. AC-008/AC-016 and AC-019 are omissions rather than falsehoods, but AC-008's is compile-breaking and AC-019's leaves an unlocked panic path in a method the spec's own api_surface describes as total.\n\nConditions 2 (no TBDs -- case-insensitive scan for TBD/TODO/FIXME/placeholder/to-be-determined returns nothing) and 4 (scope is four small files, roughly 250 lines of verbatim Rust in one cohesive module; comfortably one planning cycle) both pass. Condition 3 (error cases carry is_error_case: true) passes for what is covered -- AC-006, AC-010, AC-011, AC-013, AC-014 -- but AC-019's missing panic criterion is a gap in it. Condition 1 fails on AC-006 and AC-020.\n\nNo conditional pass. The spec is close and the tracing work behind AC-009, AC-011 and AC-013 is genuinely good and independently confirmed, but AC-006 as written makes a tester write a failing test and AC-020 as written is refuted by four lines of the source it claims to lock. Both must be corrected before planning."
}

