    }
  ],
  "overall": "fail",
  "reasoning": "Re-verified all 23 tasks from scratch against cargo 1.96.0 and the live docs, with no inheritance of the prior pass's verdict. All seven claimed fixes are genuine and I confirmed each empirically: T-018's red state (0 unexpected_cfgs warnings) is materially different from its green state (warning emitted, exit 0); T-019's fixture genuinely transitions exit 0 -> exit 101 with `error: duplicate key` at `Cargo.toml:12:1` exactly as written, and the tempfile promotion contrast parses at exit 0; T-023's deliberately-broken two-[package] fixture is genuinely rejected (rc 101, BROKEN_REJECTED) and the real assembled copy genuinely parses (exit 0) with all four python assertions passing including `len(nondev)==22`; T-023's [[bench]] caveat is factually accurate (metadata reports only lib and bin targets); all four awk pattern-anchor extractions (T-001, T-003, T-019, T-023) extract precisely the claimed content against the real current documents, with every anchor unique in-file and the Section 1 block diffing byte-clean against the fully assembled manifest; and T-020's fixed grep pattern matches cargo's real error text both in the /tmp fixture and against the real repo state left by T-011. I also independently ran T-006, T-021, T-022 and every T-007..T-016 green check -- all pass. Coverage is complete: 28/28 criteria claimed, zero orphans, zero forward dependencies, spec_hash matches the file on disk, and all five is_error_case criteria (AC-001B, AC-003B, AC-008B, AC-016, AC-025) have real reproduction tasks. No task exceeds fifteen minutes and no step is over-specified beyond what a content-lock track warrants. Verdict is fail on three blocking issues, one of which fix #7 introduced. First, T-023's new length guard is inoperative on the stated platform -- BSD `wc -l` padding makes the string comparison always false, so the step emits SOURCE BLOCK MOVED on a correct 86-line block; a guard that always fires is worse than no guard because the implementer learns to ignore it. Second, and most consequential, the plan's central discipline -- verify against the source document, not against a heredoc re-typed inside the plan -- was applied to T-001, T-003, and T-023 but not propagated to the two tasks carrying the most transcribed content. I proved this is not theoretical: injecting two wrong version pins into T-005's block passed every check in all 23 tasks, and T-005's ten lines are diffed against literally nothing, only counted. Third, lib.rs has the identical gap, and AC-015's gate-distribution half is claimed only by T-018, a task that touches no repo file at all. The fifth issue is smaller but concrete: T-020's real-repo assertion is the plan's only cwd-dependent command and fails ambiguously if run from elsewhere. Every finding above was reproduced live and every suggested fix command was executed and confirmed working before being written down."
}
